WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.601
-Our next guest
is the Super Bowl-winning

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.036
former head coach
of the Pittsburgh Steelers

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:05.938
and a member of the
Pro Football Hall of Fame.

00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:08.941
His memoir, "Heart and Steel,"
is available now.

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:11.344
Please welcome to the show
Bill Cowher.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.113
Coach Cowher, I -- Look.
I'm just going to be honest.

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:16.782
I'm really excited you're here.

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:18.084
-Hey, Seth,
I didn't get the dress code.

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.687
Was I supposed to wear
a tank top?

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.089
I don't know. I wasn't sure.
-No, this is for the best.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:25.992
I think we managed
to hammer that home.

00:00:25.992 --> 00:00:27.360
-Okay.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.128
-So, this is a really cool thing

00:00:29.128 --> 00:00:31.297
that you were inducted
into the hall of fame,

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:33.133
but because of the pandemic,
you're actually going to get

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:34.834
to the ceremony this August.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:39.171
Have you gotten to at least
put on the gold jacket yet?

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:40.506
-I have put on the gold jacket.

00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:43.042
I got it earlier.
It fit perfectly.

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:46.445
My number on the inside is 331.

00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:48.514
So, forever, I'll be number 331

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:50.249
in the Canton
Pro Football Hall of Fame,

00:00:50.249 --> 00:00:51.851
as I sit in there with my bust.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:53.686
It's a team
I can't get cut from, Seth.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.654
I'm on it
for the rest of my life.

00:00:55.654 --> 00:00:57.056
-You know, this is --

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:58.357
I knew a fair amount
about your career,

00:00:58.357 --> 00:00:59.659
but I really enjoyed
reading the book.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:02.228
One of the most
fascinating things to me --

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.863
you were playing football,
you know, you had been,

00:01:04.863 --> 00:01:07.032
obviously, plagued by injuries
to some degree.

00:01:07.032 --> 00:01:09.068
But you were still
an active football player

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:11.837
when you decided to take less
money and get into coaching.

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:13.439
Obviously,
that worked out for you,

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.441
but that must have been
an incredible leap of faith

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:18.177
to make as a young guy.

00:01:18.177 --> 00:01:19.679
-Yeah, it really was.

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.915
If you looked through the book,
Seth, I was probably more public

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:24.483
than I've ever been in doing so.

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:26.352
And as I sat down
with this book,

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:28.922
I talked about --
it's not just a football book.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.190
It's about football, family,

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.824
and one's life journey
of lessons learned.

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:34.393
And along the way, I had to make
a lot of those decisions

00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:36.862
we all have to make when you
reach that fork in the road.

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:38.231
Yogi Berra says,
"Just take it."

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:40.133
I think you've got to
put some thought into it.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:43.269
So, I was given an opportunity
by Marty Schottenheimer

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:46.072
to go with him,
to get into coaching,

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:47.740
and I never looked back.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.609
-As you mentioned,
this isn't just a football book.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:52.945
It's really a beautiful book
about family.

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:55.181
You talk about
the wonderful family you have,

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:57.416
as well as some really
difficult loss.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.918
Your father, your wife
both passed away

00:01:59.918 --> 00:02:01.587
in a really small
period of time.

00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:06.859
Was it hard to go back
and write those stories down?

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:08.361
-It really was.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:10.230
I mean, I sat there and had to
find the perfect collaborator

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.965
to write that with,
and I got Michael Holley.

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:13.566
And he was that.

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:15.234
He was able to pull
more stories out of me

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:18.371
than a licensed therapist,
to be quite honest with you.

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:19.706
We sat there and talked about

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.207
how we're going to
write this book, and he said,

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:22.575
"Let's just do it
chronologically."

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:25.178
So, as I sat down and went
through the periods of time

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:27.280
in my life --
as you talk about growing up

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.015
in Pittsburgh,
going back to Pittsburgh,

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:30.783
playing in the National
Football League,

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.718
then coaching in
the National Football League,

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:35.688
the reason I stepped down,
got into TV,

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.656
and then, as you stated,
in 2010, a period of time

00:02:37.656 --> 00:02:39.559
where I lost my father
and my wife

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:41.494
within three months'
period of time,

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:45.598
I found myself laughing,
crying, smiling,

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.433
and just thinking and reflecting

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:48.934
on a lot of things
that took place in my life

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:50.636
and the mentors
are a big part of it --

00:02:50.636 --> 00:02:54.173
that being Marty Schottenheimer,
Dan Rooney, and my father.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:55.708
And, really,
the women in my life,

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.309
as well,
have been a big part of it.

00:02:57.309 --> 00:02:58.411
Because I have three daughters,

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:00.313
my beautiful wife
that passed away, Kaye,

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.415
and my current wife today, V.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:04.550
-You talk about the fact,
you know,

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:05.918
you grew up in Pittsburgh,

00:03:05.918 --> 00:03:08.120
everyone around you was
a Pittsburgh Steelers fan.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.290
You're 34 years old when you get
the head-coaching job,

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.058
you know, which is so --
when you think about it now,

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:15.327
so incredibly young to
take on that responsibility.

00:03:15.327 --> 00:03:18.697
You have a great story in
the book about a season ending

00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:22.001
and going out to dinner,
and your dad kind of forgetting

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:24.270
that you were the actual coach
of the Steelers at the time.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.206
-It was. It was at the end
of the '92 season.

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:30.376
And you remember the '92 season.
Neil O'Donnell got hurt.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:33.512
I put Bubby Brister in for,
like, the last five games.

00:03:33.512 --> 00:03:35.881
And Bubby played better
as we went on, right?

00:03:35.881 --> 00:03:37.950
Neil got ready.
We had a bye week.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.119
And I thought a player
never loses his job to injury.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.921
So Neil came in
and played the playoff game

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.155
against the Buffalo Bills.

00:03:43.155 --> 00:03:45.325
It was not one
of his better games.

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:47.893
The next weekend, we go over
to my dad's house in Crafton,

00:03:47.893 --> 00:03:51.163
the same place we always
had Sunday afternoon dinners.

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:52.698
My brothers are there,
my kids are there,

00:03:52.698 --> 00:03:54.600
everybody takes
their place at a seat.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.201
And it's kind of hectic.
Things are going on.

00:03:56.201 --> 00:03:58.071
My dad gets up out of his seat,

00:03:58.071 --> 00:03:59.838
goes over and gets a beer
out of the kitchen,

00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:02.208
and he goes,
"How about them Steelers?

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.477
How dumb were them to sit there
and start Neil O'Donnell?

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:06.379
He hadn't played in a month,
and Bubby Brister

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:07.679
is getting better and better."

00:04:07.679 --> 00:04:09.048
I go, "Dad."
He goes, "What?"

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:10.683
"Them Steelers?"
I go, "That was me,

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.317
your son,
that made that decision."

00:04:12.317 --> 00:04:13.652
He kind of stopped and he goes,

00:04:13.652 --> 00:04:15.254
"Well, I'm sure you had
a good reason for it."

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:16.622
Didn't even ask me.

00:04:16.622 --> 00:04:19.058
I was just like, "Okay.
I guess I was wrong again."

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:20.826
Then he went on
to another subject.

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:22.761
I thought, "Wow.
You'll never take the fan

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:24.296
out of Pittsburghers."

00:04:24.296 --> 00:04:25.798
-Well, you know, my father --

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:27.900
that's why I love the Steelers
as much as I do.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.835
And I was talking to him
about this book,

00:04:29.835 --> 00:04:31.837
and I said I don't know
if this is healthy or not,

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:33.972
but there are games

00:04:33.972 --> 00:04:35.675
that when Bill writes
about them in the book,

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:38.544
I think that if I wrote
my autobiography,

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.846
I would actually have more pages
about the game.

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:43.082
That's how traumatic
it was for me.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.818
Is it -- When you look
back at your career,

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:50.756
do you remember the wins more
or the losses more?

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:53.726
-Seth, I remember every
AFC Championship loss.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:56.462
And I know you were actually
at the '94 Championship loss

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.597
that we had against
the San Diego Chargers.

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:00.533
Do you remember
the temperature that day?

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:03.235
-I remember it was very cold.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:07.039
-No, it was very warm.
It was like 60 degrees.

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:10.175
-Oh, I think I meant that my
heart got very cold at the end.

00:05:10.175 --> 00:05:14.313
-[ Laughs ] It was 60 degrees
in the middle of January.

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.181
We're playing
the San Diego Chargers.

00:05:16.181 --> 00:05:19.751
That was a bad -- I just thought
that's a bad omen to start with.

00:05:19.751 --> 00:05:23.222
And we got down to it,
and the bottom line is,

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.624
we had a chance to throw
the ball into the end zone

00:05:25.624 --> 00:05:27.994
to win the championship
and go down --

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:29.695
and go try to win
one for the thumb.

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:31.130
It fell incomplete.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.633
The San Diego Chargers
go to the Super Bowl.

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:35.968
And that happens
to be the same year

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.637
that I had my 20th
high-school class reunion.

00:05:38.637 --> 00:05:41.974
And I go back to my 20th
high-school class reunion,

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:43.876
head coach
of the Pittsburgh Steelers.

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:45.711
And I'm sitting there
thinking, okay,

00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:48.281
we came off a pretty good
season, in a championship game,

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:49.948
three straight years
in the playoffs.

00:05:49.948 --> 00:05:51.550
What are we going to do
when we have the reunion?

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.654
I think I'll just do a --
just run by, stop in, stop out.

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:56.388
We're going to do it
on a cruise.

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:57.857
I go, "A what?"
"A cruise."

00:05:57.857 --> 00:06:00.426
So we get on this boat.
It's going to be a boat.

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:01.961
The boat takes off.

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:04.496
Now I'm on a river cruise for
three hours with all the guys

00:06:04.496 --> 00:06:06.632
I went to high school with,
all telling me the things

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.634
I did wrong
in the San Diego Charger game,

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:10.536
asking for tickets
for the next year.

00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.705
And I'm thinking,
"I can't get off this boat."

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:14.507
So I had to be very nice
to everybody

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.608
and cordial with everybody.
I'm like, "Yeah, you're right."

00:06:16.608 --> 00:06:18.143
"Absolutely right."

00:06:18.143 --> 00:06:19.812
"I should have thought
about that."

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.281
"Yeah, you told me that
back in high school, yep."

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:26.052
-[ Laughs ]
I certainly, you know --

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:30.022
I was with my family when you
won the Super Bowl in 2005.

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:32.625
It's one of my favorite
moments with my family.

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:35.261
Obviously, you got to celebrate
it with your family, too.

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:37.263
Does it make it easier,

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:40.566
looking back at your tenure
in Pittsburgh

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:43.936
because you did get to bring
the Lombardi back?

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.338
-Yeah, it did.
It meant so much to me, Seth,

00:06:46.338 --> 00:06:49.307
to be able to hand that trophy
to Dan Rooney,

00:06:49.307 --> 00:06:50.676
you know, after 14 years,

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:53.078
because he had entrusted
so much faith in me

00:06:53.078 --> 00:06:55.681
and stayed with me through
a lot of difficult times

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:57.483
and a lot of
disappointing losses.

00:06:57.483 --> 00:07:01.754
I talk about the '94 game,
'97 against the Denver Broncos,

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:05.158
2001 against
the New England Patriots,

00:07:05.158 --> 00:07:07.260
and then 2004,
just the year before,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.061
against the Patriots again.

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:11.263
And we finally tried to get
there and get over the hump

00:07:11.263 --> 00:07:12.831
and then get over the hump
and go back

00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:14.533
and then be able to hand that
to Mr. Rooney

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:17.669
was one of
the most gratifying --

00:07:17.669 --> 00:07:21.207
I would say the most
gratifying moment in my career.

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:23.976
-I feel as though once a coach
leaves Pittsburgh,

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:26.311
especially someone
who is local like you,

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:29.047
you are now only
looked back on with love.

00:07:29.047 --> 00:07:31.617
You are a beloved figure
in Pittsburgh.

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:33.886
But in the time
that you were there,

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:35.187
even though
you were a local kid,

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:38.157
even though your teams
had a lot of success,

00:07:38.157 --> 00:07:40.626
is it true that you had
a different way of getting gas

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:44.330
during the season
and out of season?

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:45.664
-Yes, it is.

00:07:45.664 --> 00:07:47.933
You know, the longer
you stay in Pittsburgh,

00:07:47.933 --> 00:07:50.036
you find out that people
get closer to you.

00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:52.537
You start to build
a little bit of a friendship.

00:07:52.537 --> 00:07:54.574
And then people feel like
they can feel very comfortable

00:07:54.574 --> 00:07:56.942
giving you advice
and start telling you the things

00:07:56.942 --> 00:07:58.611
that can help you
get over the hump.

00:07:58.611 --> 00:08:00.345
"I know exactly
what you need to do."

00:08:00.345 --> 00:08:03.182
So it got to the point where --
you know what?

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:05.083
If I had to stop and get gas,
I waited --

00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:07.954
If we won, I got gas that week
during the day.

00:08:07.954 --> 00:08:10.056
Not a problem.
"Hey, Coach, great game."

00:08:10.056 --> 00:08:13.926
If we lost, I learned to get gas
at night and go to self-serve.

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:15.428
I'd get there
and pull my hat down.

00:08:15.428 --> 00:08:17.196
I didn't need to hear
about all the things

00:08:17.196 --> 00:08:19.698
that we should've been doing
and the things we didn't do.

00:08:19.698 --> 00:08:22.067
So, yes, I was actually --

00:08:22.067 --> 00:08:24.303
I was kind of just
acclimating my lifestyle

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:26.138
to whether we'd win or lose.

00:08:26.138 --> 00:08:28.106
-Last thing, "Cahr Pahr" --

00:08:28.106 --> 00:08:30.242
For people who aren't
from Pittsburgh,

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:33.245
they'll probably think
those are just nonsense words.

00:08:33.245 --> 00:08:35.848
Do you want to tell
everybody what Cahr Pahr is?

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:38.116
-Well, "Cahr" is, like,
another way

00:08:38.116 --> 00:08:40.219
for a Pittsburgher,
kind of a Yinzer --

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:42.588
Like, yinz, Cowher --
"Hey, Cahr."

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:44.223
You know, like, Cowher Power.

00:08:44.223 --> 00:08:47.425
"Yeah, it's Cahr Pahr Hour."

00:08:47.425 --> 00:08:50.229
We don't pronounce our words --
That's one of the things

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:52.231
I had to get over
going into TV, Seth,

00:08:52.231 --> 00:08:54.867
was learning how
to enunciate words again.

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:57.937
-Well, you've done
a very fine job with it,

00:08:57.937 --> 00:09:00.172
and you've written
a very fine book.

00:09:00.172 --> 00:09:01.674
Really, really appreciate
the time.

00:09:01.674 --> 00:09:03.909
Thanks so much
for being here, Coach.

00:09:03.909 --> 00:09:05.510
-Thanks for having me, Seth.

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:07.646
-"Heart and Steel"
is available everywhere now,

00:09:07.646 --> 00:09:08.981
but please remember to support

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:10.349
local and independent
bookstores.

